Since $1\text{ hour}=60\text{ minutes}$, we know that both $\dfrac{1\text{ hour}}{60\text{ minutes}}$ and $\dfrac{60\text{ minutes}}{1\text{ hour}}$ equal $1$.

So we can multiply by either of those fractions to help us convert units, since multiplying by $1$ doesn't change value. In this problem, we multiply by $\blueD{\dfrac{1\text{ hour}}{60\text{ minutes}}}$ since the quantity we want to convert is in $\text{minutes}$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}40\,\cancel{\text{minutes}}\cdot \blueD{\dfrac{1\text{ hour}}{60\,\,\cancel{\text{minutes}}}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{40}{60}\,\text{hours}
\end{align}$
